export const F1={title:'How the Family Medicine Physician event works',hook:'This event asks you to do what family physicians do every day: listen to people’s stories, understand the system they live in and explain it clearly to others. You will research, interview a family physician and a second person, teach your peers and then present that same lesson to judges in ten minutes.',objectives:['Describe the steps of the event from research to the judged presentation.','Explain the Interview Verification Form upload and the 15-point deduction.','Break down the presentation rubric and where points concentrate.','Plan a realistic timeline that includes AAFP connection lead time.'],sections:[
['From research to judges',[
'Family Medicine Physician, sponsored by the **American Academy of Family Physicians (AAFP)**, is a solo event for Secondary and Postsecondary/Collegiate members. Competitors **research** six learning objectives, conduct **at least two interviews**, deliver a **live peer-to-peer presentation**—“Who is a Family Medicine Physician?” or a title of their choosing—and then give **the same presentation to judges**. New for 2026–27, **prep time has been removed** and the presentation is **ten minutes**. Competitors bring photo ID, any battery-powered presentation technology and optional notecards; electronic notecards must be on a different device from the one shown to judges.',
order('The event sequence',['Research the six learning objectives using the AAFP landing page and other sources','Request AAFP interview connections early if needed (at least 4 weeks)','Interview a family physician and a second eligible person','Complete and upload the Interview Verification Form by May 15','Build a creative, educational peer presentation','Deliver it live to a peer audience','Present the same content to judges in 10 minutes'])]],
['The six learning objectives',[
'The guidelines list six learning objectives, and the rubric scores each: **(a) demand and occupational outlook**—why now is the time to consider family medicine; **(b) span of medical education and career options**—medical school, residency, dual degrees, fellowships, Certificates of Added Qualifications, practice settings and emphases; **(c) work, lifestyle and financial implications**—benefits, challenges, the health care team, work-life balance, education costs, salaries, signing bonuses, scholarships and loan forgiveness; **(d) importance of primary care and preventive medicine to achieve health equity**—the impact on patients, families and communities and family physicians as advocates; **(e) the WHY**—the story of why the interviewed physician chose this path; and **(f) “Is family medicine right for me?”**—why it may or may not fit you.',
match('Match the learning objective to its focus',[['a','Demand and occupational outlook'],['b','Medical education and career options'],['c','Work, lifestyle and financial implications'],['d','Primary care, prevention and health equity'],['e','The story of WHY the physician chose the career'],['f','Whether family medicine is right for the competitor']])]],
['The Interview Verification Form',[
'Once interviewees agree, the competitor completes the **Interview Verification Form**, with each interviewee’s name and title, interview date(s), practice or hospital name and address, email address and **signature**. By signing, interviewees consent to post-event communication from the AAFP unless they check the opt-out box. The form is uploaded as **one PDF** to the HOSA Digital Upload System by **May 15 at midnight EST**. It is **not pre-judged**; staff check compliance, and **failure to upload by the deadline results in a 15-point deduction** in tabulations. As with all uploads, a state-level upload does not replace the separate ILC upload.',
chk('What is the penalty for not uploading the Interview Verification Form by May 15?',['A 15-point deduction','Disqualification from all HOSA events','A 5-point deduction','No penalty because it is not pre-judged'],'The form is checked for compliance; missing it costs 15 points.')]],
['The presentation rubric',[
'The rubric rewards content most heavily:',
{t:'table',caption:'Family Medicine Physician presentation rubric',head:['Section','Item','Points'],rows:[['A. Overview','Interviews and peer presentation confirmed at the start','5'],['B. Content','Demand and occupational outlook','10'],['B. Content','Medical education and career options','10'],['B. Content','Work, lifestyle and financial implications','10'],['B. Content','Primary care, prevention and health equity','10'],['B. Content','A powerful story of WHY the physician chose the career','20'],['B. Content','Is family medicine right for me?','20'],['B. Content','Distinctive, captivating, unique','15'],['C. Organization','Flow and transitions; opening; closing','5 each (15)'],['D. Materials','Visual aids, tools and technology','20'],['E. Delivery','Voice; stage presence; diction, pronunciation and grammar','5 each (15)']]},
'These items total **150 points**. The two story-driven items—the physician’s **WHY** and **“Is family medicine right for me?”**—are worth 40 points together, more than the four research topics combined. Ties are broken by the highest-value rating-sheet items, so storytelling, visual aids and personal reflection decide close results.',
sort('Which rubric section does each element belong to?',['Content (B)','Organization, materials or delivery (C–E)'],[['Explaining residency and fellowships',0],['The physician’s emotional story',0],['A memorable closing statement',1],['Slides that help tell the story',1],['Varying volume and pausing',1],['Why the time is now for family medicine',0]])]],
['Planning the timeline',[
'Work backward from your conference date and **May 15**. If you do not have connections, complete the **AAFP online form** to be matched with a family physician and a medical student—HOSA recommends **at least four weeks** of lead time. You may also find interviewees through your HOSA advisor, health science classroom, family, friends or community partners. Schedule the peer presentation after the interviews so you can include the stories, and leave time to revise based on audience reactions. Rehearse the judged version with a timer, including the verification statement at the start.',
kase('A tight schedule',{a:{text:'It is April 20, and you have not found a family physician to interview. What is the best step?',choices:[['Submit the AAFP connection form today and ask your advisor and community contacts at the same time','b','Correct. Use every channel immediately; AAFP recommends four weeks.',true],['Interview two medical students instead','b','One interview must be with a family medicine physician; both may not be medical students.',false]]},b:{text:'A retired family physician from your community agrees. Is that acceptable?',choices:[['Yes—retired family medicine physicians are acceptable','c','Correct. The guidelines allow a retired family physician.',true],['No—only practicing physicians count','c','Retired family physicians are explicitly acceptable.',false]]},c:{text:'You interview the retired physician and a medical student, have both sign the verification form and upload it on May 5.',end:true}})]],
["Organizing your research",["The guidelines point competitors to an **AAFP summary landing page** of recommended reading and viewing for this event, plus the *Start With Why* talk; competitors may add sources of their own choosing. Begin there, then confirm and update facts with primary sources: the AAFP, the American Board of Family Medicine, the AAMC, the Bureau of Labor Statistics, the National Resident Matching Program and federal programs such as the National Health Service Corps. Because the presentation is scored on “overall understanding,” you need enough depth to explain each objective in your own words and answer the obvious follow-up question in your head before an audience asks it.","Keep a **research notebook** organized by objective (a–f). For each fact, record the source, the date and a one-sentence explanation of why it matters. Mark which facts you want to confirm in the interviews and which questions only an interview can answer. When you build the presentation, this notebook becomes your outline and lets you cite sources on your slides accurately. Avoid copying slides or scripts from other sources; the distinctiveness item rewards original work.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Research source or interview question?","buckets":["Research with authoritative sources","Ask in the interview"],"items":[["Length of family medicine residency",0],["National shortage projections",0],["Why the physician chose family medicine",1],["How the physician balances call with family life",1],["Requirements for NHSC loan repayment",0],["A moment that made the physician proud",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Research, two interviews, a live peer presentation and the same 10-minute presentation to judges; prep time was removed for 2026–27.','Upload the signed Interview Verification Form as one PDF by May 15 or lose 15 points.','The rubric totals 150; the WHY story and “Is it right for me?” are worth 20 each, visual aids 20 and distinctiveness 15.'],terms:[['Peer-to-peer presentation','A live presentation delivered to a student audience before competition.'],['Interview Verification Form','Signed form identifying interviewees, uploaded by May 15.'],['Learning objectives','The six content areas the presentation must cover.']],mcqs:[q('How long is the presentation to judges in 2026–27?','10 minutes','5 minutes','15 minutes','7 minutes with 3 minutes of prep','Prep time was removed.'),q('What happens if the Interview Verification Form is not uploaded on time?','A 15-point deduction','Automatic first place','No penalty','The interviews are cancelled','It is checked for compliance.'),q('Which organization sponsors the event?','American Academy of Family Physicians','American Medical Association','American Hospital Association','National Institutes of Health','AAFP also helps connect interviewees.'),q('How many interviews are required?','At least two, with two separate people','One','Three','Five','One must be with a family physician.'),q('Which rubric items are worth 20 points each?','The WHY story, Is family medicine right for me, and visual aids','Voice and stage presence','Opening and closing','Demand and outlook only','Story and reflection weigh heavily.'),q('How far in advance does HOSA recommend contacting AAFP for interview connections?','At least 4 weeks','1 day','1 week','6 months','Lead time ensures connections.'),q('The presentation to judges should be:','The same presentation given to peers','A different presentation','A written report','A recorded video only','Judges see what peers saw.'),q('Electronic notecards must be:','On a different device from the one shown to judges','Shown to judges','On the presentation laptop','Printed on slides','Only presentation aids may be shown.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the requirements and scoring of the Family Medicine Physician event and outline a timeline that ensures you meet every requirement.',rubric:[['Event components',['research','interview','peer','presentation','judges','10 minutes']],['Verification and deadlines',['verification form','may 15','pdf','15-point','signature']],['Rubric emphasis',['why','right for me','20','visual','150']],['Timeline',['aafp','4 weeks','schedule','rehearse','timer']]],model:'The event asks me to research six learning objectives—demand and outlook, education and career options, work, lifestyle and finances, primary care and health equity, the physician’s WHY and whether family medicine is right for me—interview a family physician and a second person (a medical student or another family physician), present live to my peers and then give the same presentation to judges in ten minutes. I must have both interviewees sign the Interview Verification Form and upload it as one PDF by May 15 or lose 15 points. The rubric totals 150 points, and the WHY story, the personal fit reflection and visual aids are worth 20 each, with distinctiveness worth 15, so storytelling and creativity matter most. My timeline would be: in January, research the objectives and submit the AAFP connection form at least four weeks before I need interviews; in February and March, conduct the interviews and get the form signed; by early April, build the presentation and deliver it to my HOSA chapter; upload the form well before May 15; and rehearse the judged version with a timer, starting with the verification statement.'},sources:[RULES,AAFP,UPLOAD]};

export const F2={title:'What is family medicine?',hook:'A family physician might deliver a baby in the morning, manage an older adult’s diabetes and heart failure at noon, counsel a teenager about anxiety in the afternoon and visit a nursing home on the way home. Family medicine is the specialty of breadth: care for the whole person, at every age, in the context of family and community.',objectives:['Define family medicine and its core principles.','Describe the history of the specialty.','Explain the scope of care family physicians provide.','Distinguish family medicine from other primary care specialties.'],sections:[
['Defining the specialty',[
'**Family medicine** is the medical specialty that provides **continuing, comprehensive health care for individuals and families** across **all ages, sexes, organ systems and disease entities**. Its core principles are **first-contact care** (the place patients go first), **continuity** (a long-term relationship with the same physician or team), **comprehensiveness** (most health needs handled in one place), **coordination** (guiding patients through specialists, hospitals and community resources) and a focus on the **whole person** within their **family and community**. Family physicians emphasize **prevention**, manage **acute and chronic illness**, address **behavioral health** and recognize how home, work and culture affect health.',
flip('Core principles of family medicine',[['First contact','Patients come here first for new problems'],['Continuity','A relationship over years, often across generations'],['Comprehensiveness','Care for most problems at every age'],['Coordination','Guides referrals and transitions between settings'],['Whole-person care','Considers mind, body, family and community'],['Prevention','Screening, immunizations and healthy habits']])]],
['A short history',[
'In the early twentieth century, most physicians were **general practitioners** who cared for entire families. As medicine became more specialized after World War II, the number of generalists fell and access suffered. The **American Academy of General Practice** formed in **1947** and later became the **American Academy of Family Physicians (AAFP)**. In **1969**, family practice was recognized as a specialty, with the **American Board of Family Practice**—now the **American Board of Family Medicine (ABFM)**—certifying physicians who completed residency training. The new specialty required residency training and recertification, emphasizing lifelong learning. Osteopathic family physicians are certified through the American Osteopathic Board of Family Physicians. Today family physicians form one of the largest groups of primary care physicians in the United States and provide a large share of care in **rural and underserved** areas.',
order('Milestones of the specialty',['General practitioners care for whole families','Specialization grows after World War II','American Academy of General Practice founded (1947)','Family practice recognized as a specialty (1969)','Board renamed American Board of Family Medicine','Family medicine anchors primary care and the medical home'])]],
['Scope of care',[
'The scope of family medicine is wide and adapts to community needs. Family physicians provide **well-child care and immunizations**, **adolescent health**, **women’s health** and contraception, **prenatal care** and, for many, **delivering babies**; **chronic disease management** (hypertension, diabetes, asthma, COPD, heart disease); **mental and behavioral health** (depression, anxiety, substance use); **geriatric care** and **end-of-life care**; **acute care** for infections and injuries; and **office procedures** such as skin biopsies, joint injections, laceration repair and IUD placement. In rural areas they may work in emergency departments, hospitals and nursing homes. They treat most problems themselves and refer when specialist care is needed, then coordinate follow-up.',
sort('Commonly within a family physician’s scope?',['Commonly within scope','Usually referred to a specialist'],[['Managing type 2 diabetes',0],['Well-child visits and vaccines',0],['Skin biopsy of a suspicious mole',0],['Open-heart surgery',1],['Treating depression with therapy referral and medication',0],['Complex brain tumor resection',1],['Prenatal care',0],['Kidney transplant',1]])]],
['Family medicine and other primary care',[
'Primary care in the United States includes **family medicine**, **general internal medicine** (adults only), **general pediatrics** (children and adolescents) and, for some, **obstetrics and gynecology**, along with nurse practitioners and physician assistants in primary care. Family medicine is the only one of these specialties that trains physicians to care for **patients of every age and both sexes across all organ systems**, including maternity care. That breadth allows one physician to care for several generations of the same family, understanding shared genetics, environment and relationships—a perspective that strengthens diagnosis and prevention.',
match('Match the primary care field to its patients',[['Family medicine','All ages and both sexes, including maternity care'],['General internal medicine','Adults'],['General pediatrics','Infants, children and adolescents'],['Obstetrics and gynecology','Reproductive and pregnancy care'],['Geriatrics fellowship','Older adults with complex needs'],['Adolescent medicine CAQ','Teenagers and young adults']])]],
['A day in family medicine',[
'A typical clinic day might include 18–25 visits of 15–30 minutes, phone and portal messages, prescription refills, reviewing laboratory results, coordinating with specialists and documentation in the **electronic health record**. Many family physicians work in a **patient-centered medical home**, where a team—nurses, medical assistants, pharmacists, behavioral health clinicians, care coordinators, nurse practitioners and physician assistants—shares responsibility for a panel of patients. Others work as hospitalists, in urgent care, in academic medicine or in public health. Interviewing a family physician about their typical day is one of the best ways to bring this scope to life for your peers.',
kase('Whole-person care',{a:{text:'A 52-year-old visits a family physician for a sore knee. His blood pressure is 158/96, and he mentions he lost his job. What does whole-person care suggest?',choices:[['Treat the knee, address the blood pressure and ask about stress, mood and insurance coverage','b','Correct. Family physicians address all of a patient’s needs in context.',true],['Treat only the knee because that is why he came','b','First-contact, comprehensive care addresses more than the chief complaint.',false]]},b:{text:'He worries he cannot afford medications. What can the physician do?',choices:[['Choose affordable generic medications and connect him with community and insurance resources','c','Correct. Coordination includes social needs.',true],['Tell him to return when he has a new job','c','Delays increase risk.',false]]},c:{text:'At follow-up, his blood pressure is improving, his knee is better and a care coordinator helped him enroll in coverage.',end:true}})]],
["Family medicine around the world and in the community",["Primary care is a global idea. The **1978 Declaration of Alma-Ata**, adopted at an international conference organized by the World Health Organization and UNICEF, called primary health care the key to “Health for All,” and the **2018 Declaration of Astana** renewed that commitment. Family doctors are the backbone of primary care in many countries, and the **World Organization of Family Doctors (WONCA)** connects family physicians internationally. Countries with strong primary care systems tend to achieve better population health at lower cost, an argument family physicians use when advocating for investment in primary care in the United States.","At home, many family physicians practice **community-oriented primary care**: they look beyond individual patients to the health of the community they serve—identifying local problems such as high rates of uncontrolled diabetes or teen vaping, designing programs with community members and measuring results. They serve as school and team physicians, medical directors for nursing homes, volunteers at free clinics and leaders in local public health. This community role is part of what makes the specialty distinct, and it connects naturally to objective (d) on health equity.",{"t":"check","q":"Which 1978 declaration called primary health care the key to “Health for All”?","options":["Declaration of Alma-Ata","Declaration of Independence","Geneva Convention","Declaration of Helsinki"],"why":"It was renewed by the 2018 Declaration of Astana."}]]
],takeaways:['Family medicine provides continuing, comprehensive care for all ages, sexes and organ systems in the context of family and community.','Recognized as a specialty in 1969; the AAFP began as the American Academy of General Practice in 1947; the ABFM certifies family physicians.','Scope includes prevention, chronic disease, behavioral health, maternity care and procedures, with coordination across the team.'],terms:[['Primary care','First-contact, continuous, comprehensive and coordinated care.'],['Continuity of care','A long-term relationship between patient and physician.'],['Patient-centered medical home','Team-based primary care model coordinating a patient’s needs.']],mcqs:[q('Family medicine provides care for:','All ages, sexes and organ systems','Adults only','Children only','Surgical patients only','Breadth is its defining feature.'),q('In what year was family practice recognized as a specialty?','1969','1947','1910','2005','The American Board of Family Practice was formed.'),q('The AAFP began as the:','American Academy of General Practice','American College of Surgeons','American Board of Internal Medicine','American Nurses Association','It was founded in 1947.'),q('Which principle describes a long-term physician–patient relationship?','Continuity','Coordination','Specialization','Referral','Continuity improves outcomes.'),q('Which specialty cares only for adults?','General internal medicine','Family medicine','Pediatrics','Obstetrics','Family medicine includes children.'),q('Which is commonly within a family physician’s scope?','Prenatal care and skin biopsies','Open-heart surgery','Neurosurgery','Organ transplantation','Many perform office procedures.'),q('A patient-centered medical home is:','A team-based model that coordinates a patient’s care','A nursing home','A hospital ward','A pharmacy','The team shares responsibility.'),q('Which organization certifies family physicians today?','American Board of Family Medicine','AAFP','HOSA','State hospital associations','It was renamed from the American Board of Family Practice.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain to a group of peers what makes family medicine different from other medical specialties, using its core principles, history and scope of care.',rubric:[['Definition and principles',['comprehensive','continuity','first contact','coordinat','whole person']],['History',['1969','general practi','1947','board','specialty']],['Scope of care',['all ages','prevention','chronic','maternity','procedure','behavioral']],['Comparison to other specialties',['internal medicine','pediatrics','adults','children','generations']]],model:'Family medicine is the specialty that provides continuing, comprehensive care for people of all ages, sexes and organ systems in the context of their families and communities. Its principles are first-contact care, continuity over years, comprehensiveness, coordination with specialists and community resources, and whole-person care focused on prevention. It grew out of general practice: as medicine specialized after World War II, the American Academy of General Practice formed in 1947, and in 1969 family practice became a recognized specialty with its own board, now the American Board of Family Medicine. Family physicians provide well-child care and vaccines, adolescent and women’s health, prenatal care and often deliveries, chronic disease management, behavioral health, geriatric and end-of-life care, and office procedures such as biopsies and joint injections. Unlike internal medicine, which treats adults, or pediatrics, which treats children, family medicine can care for an entire family across generations, which helps physicians understand shared genetics, environment and relationships.'},sources:[RULES,AAFP,ABFM]};

export const F3={title:'Demand and occupational outlook (Objective a)',hook:'Many communities do not have enough primary care physicians, and the U.S. population is getting older and living longer with chronic disease. Learning objective (a) asks you to show why the need for family physicians is growing—and why now is the time to consider the career.',objectives:['Explain the drivers of demand for family physicians.','Use authoritative data on physician workforce projections and employment.','Describe shortages in rural and underserved areas.','Explain why “now” is a strong time to consider family medicine.'],sections:[
['What drives demand',[
'Several forces increase the need for family physicians. The population is **growing and aging**: older adults visit physicians more often and have more chronic conditions such as hypertension, diabetes, arthritis and heart disease. **Chronic disease** now accounts for most health care spending, and primary care is where it is prevented and managed. Many physicians are themselves **nearing retirement**. Efforts to expand insurance coverage and emphasize **prevention and value-based care** rely on primary care. Meanwhile, fewer medical students historically chose primary care than specialties with higher pay, creating gaps. Research shows that areas with more primary care physicians have better population health outcomes, including lower mortality, which makes the workforce a public health issue.',
sort('Driver of demand or not?',['Increases demand for family physicians','Not a driver of demand'],[['An aging population with more chronic disease',0],['Physicians nearing retirement',0],['Growth of value-based, preventive care',0],['Rural communities with few clinicians',0],['A decrease in the number of older adults',1],['Fewer people living with chronic conditions',1]])]],
['Reading workforce data',[
'Authoritative sources make your outlook section credible. The **Association of American Medical Colleges (AAMC)** publishes physician workforce projections; its 2024 report projected a national shortage of **up to about 86,000 physicians by 2036**, with primary care among the largest gaps. The **Bureau of Labor Statistics** publishes employment and wage estimates for **family medicine physicians** (occupation code 29-1215) and outlook information for physicians and surgeons in the *Occupational Outlook Handbook*. The **National Resident Matching Program (NRMP)** reports how many family medicine residency positions are offered and filled each year. When presenting numbers, cite the **source and year**, use a simple chart rather than a wall of statistics and explain what the number means for patients.',
{t:'example',title:'Turning a statistic into meaning',prompt:'You find a projection that the U.S. could be short tens of thousands of physicians in the next decade, with primary care among the largest gaps. How could you present this to peers?',steps:['State the source and year clearly.','Translate the number into human terms, such as patients without a regular doctor.','Connect it to your community or the interviewee’s experience.','End with why this creates opportunity for students now.'],answer:'“According to the AAMC’s 2024 projections, the U.S. could be short up to 86,000 physicians by 2036—and primary care is one of the biggest gaps. Dr. Lee told me her clinic has a three-month wait for new patients. That gap is exactly why students in this room are needed.”'}]],
['Shortages where people live',[
'National averages hide local shortages. The federal government designates **Health Professional Shortage Areas (HPSAs)** for primary care, dental and mental health where there are too few clinicians for the population. Many are **rural**, where family physicians may be the only physicians for miles and provide emergency, hospital and obstetric care. Urban neighborhoods with high poverty, **tribal communities** and areas with many uninsured residents also face shortages. Shortages lead to longer travel times, delayed care, more emergency department visits and worse control of chronic disease. Family medicine residencies increasingly offer **rural training tracks**, and programs such as the **National Health Service Corps** offer loan repayment for service in HPSAs.',
chk('What is a Health Professional Shortage Area (HPSA)?',['A federally designated area with too few clinicians for its population','A hospital ward with no beds','A medical school with no students','A region with too many physicians'],'HPSAs guide programs like NHSC loan repayment.')]],
['Why now?',[
'Learning objective (a) specifically asks why **now** is the time to consider family medicine. Make the case with evidence and examples: demand is rising faster than supply; health systems and communities are actively recruiting family physicians, often with **signing bonuses** and **loan repayment**; payment models are shifting toward **prevention and outcomes**, which rewards primary care; **telehealth** and team-based care are expanding how family physicians work; and the career offers wide flexibility in settings and emphases. Pair the data with your interviewee’s perspective—for example, how many job offers they or their residents receive, or how their community has changed.',
match('Match the evidence to the argument',[['AAMC shortage projections','Supply is not keeping up with need'],['HPSA designations','Many communities lack primary care'],['Signing bonuses and loan repayment','Employers are competing for family physicians'],['Value-based payment','Health systems reward prevention'],['Rural training tracks','Training is expanding where need is greatest'],['Interviewee’s new-patient wait list','Local evidence of demand']])]],
['Presenting the outlook to peers',[
'Peers may not know what “primary care shortage” means, so make it concrete. Use a short story, a map of shortage areas in your state, a simple chart or an interactive question (“How long do you think it takes to get a new-patient appointment?”). Keep statistics to two or three strong points with sources on the slide. Connect the outlook to the audience’s future: the students in the room could fill these roles. Judges award full points when it is “evident the competitor researched and understands this topic and why the time is now.”',
kase('Choosing the right evidence',{a:{text:'Your slide lists eight statistics in small text. A peer reviewer says it is confusing. What should you change?',choices:[['Choose two or three key numbers with sources and explain each in plain language','b','Correct. Clear, cited evidence is more persuasive.',true],['Add more statistics to look thorough','b','More numbers make the point harder to follow.',false]]},b:{text:'How can you make the demand personal for your audience?',choices:[['Share your interviewee’s experience of patient demand in your community','c','Correct. Local stories make data meaningful.',true],['Read the statistics faster','c','Speed does not improve understanding.',false]]},c:{text:'Your revised slide shows one chart, one local fact and the physician’s quote, and peers remember the message.',end:true}})]],
["Who chooses family medicine—and why it matters",["Supply depends on how many students choose the specialty. Research on specialty choice finds that students are more likely to choose family medicine when they grew up in **rural or underserved communities**, had **strong family medicine mentors** and early clinical exposure, attended schools with a **primary care mission** or **family medicine interest groups**, and had manageable debt. Students who identify with service, relationships and community often find family medicine a natural fit. Medical schools and residencies use these findings to build **pipeline programs** that reach high school and college students—programs that recruit students from the communities that most need physicians, because physicians often return to practice near where they grew up or trained.","For your presentation, this is a powerful point: demand is not only a statistic but an invitation. HOSA members who explore family medicine through interviews, shadowing and service are exactly the students these programs seek. If your interviewee grew up in a rural town or returned to their hometown, connect their story to the outlook data to show how individual choices close shortages.",{"t":"w","kind":"match","title":"Match the factor to its effect on specialty choice","pairs":[["Rural upbringing","More likely to practice in rural areas"],["Family medicine mentor","More likely to choose family medicine"],["Family medicine interest group","Early exposure to the specialty"],["High education debt","May push students toward higher-paying fields"],["Loan repayment programs","Reduce debt pressure"],["Pipeline programs","Recruit students from underserved communities"]]}]]
],takeaways:['Demand grows with an aging population, chronic disease, physician retirements and a shift to prevention and value-based care.','Cite AAMC projections (up to about 86,000 physicians short by 2036 in its 2024 report), BLS and NRMP data with source and year.','Shortage areas (HPSAs), rural needs, recruitment incentives and new care models explain why now is the time.'],terms:[['Occupational outlook','Projected demand and employment for a career.'],['Health Professional Shortage Area','Federal designation of areas with too few clinicians.'],['Value-based care','Payment tied to quality and outcomes rather than volume.']],mcqs:[q('Which factor increases demand for family physicians?','An aging population with more chronic disease','Fewer older adults','Declining rates of chronic illness','More physicians entering primary care than needed','Older adults use more care.'),q('Which organization publishes physician workforce projections?','AAMC','NFL','FDA','EPA','The Association of American Medical Colleges.'),q('An HPSA is:','An area designated as having too few health professionals','A hospital accreditation','A medical degree','A residency program','HPSAs qualify for NHSC programs.'),q('Which program offers loan repayment for service in shortage areas?','National Health Service Corps','Medicare Part D','HOSA','The NRMP','NHSC supports clinicians in HPSAs.'),q('What does the NRMP report?','Residency positions offered and filled in the Match','Hospital profits','Drug prices','Insurance enrollment','It runs the residency Match.'),q('Why do rural areas especially need family physicians?','Family physicians often provide broad care where few physicians practice','Rural areas have more specialists','Rural residents never need care','Rural hospitals have too many physicians','Breadth is vital in rural care.'),q('Which is the best way to present outlook data to peers?','Two or three key numbers with sources explained in plain language','A slide with every statistic you found','No data at all','Data without sources','Clarity earns full points.'),q('Value-based payment rewards:','Prevention and good outcomes','Only the number of procedures','Longer hospital stays','Fewer visits regardless of outcome','This favors primary care.')],frq:{prompt:'Make the case to your peers that now is a good time to consider family medicine, using at least three drivers of demand and specific, sourced evidence.',rubric:[['Drivers of demand',['aging','chronic','retire','prevention','value-based']],['Sourced evidence',['aamc','bls','nrmp','2036','source','projection']],['Shortage areas',['rural','hpsa','underserved','access']],['Why now and opportunity',['now','signing','loan','recruit','opportunity','telehealth']]],model:'Family physicians are needed now because the U.S. population is growing older and living longer with chronic conditions like diabetes, heart disease and arthritis, which are managed in primary care; many current physicians are nearing retirement; and health systems are shifting to value-based care that rewards prevention and coordination. The AAMC’s 2024 workforce projections estimate a shortage of up to about 86,000 physicians by 2036, with primary care among the largest gaps, and the Bureau of Labor Statistics tracks strong employment for family medicine physicians. Shortages are worst in rural communities and federally designated Health Professional Shortage Areas, where patients wait longer, travel farther and use emergency departments more. As a result, employers offer signing bonuses, the National Health Service Corps offers loan repayment for service in shortage areas, residencies are adding rural training tracks and telehealth is expanding access—so students who choose family medicine today will find many opportunities and communities that need them.'},sources:[RULES,AAMC_WF,BLS_OOH,NHSC]};

export const F4={title:'Medical education and career options (Objective b)',hook:'Becoming a family physician takes about eleven years after high school—and then a lifetime of learning. Objective (b) asks you to map the path from college to medical school, the residency Match, board certification and beyond, and to show the many ways family physicians shape their careers.',objectives:['Outline the education path from undergraduate study to board certification.','Compare MD and DO degrees and the licensing examinations.','Describe dual degrees, fellowships and Certificates of Added Qualifications.','Explain practice settings and emphases in family medicine.'],sections:[
['From high school to medical school',[
'Most physicians complete a **four-year bachelor’s degree**, taking prerequisite courses such as biology, general and organic chemistry, physics, biochemistry, psychology and statistics, along with clinical and volunteer experience, research and leadership. Applicants take the **Medical College Admission Test (MCAT)** and apply to **allopathic (MD)** or **osteopathic (DO)** medical schools. Some programs offer **combined BS/MD** pathways or early assurance for high school or college students. HOSA activities, shadowing and service help students explore medicine and demonstrate commitment.',
order('The path to family medicine',['Earn a bachelor’s degree with premedical coursework','Take the MCAT and apply to medical school','Complete four years of medical school (MD or DO)','Pass licensing examinations (USMLE or COMLEX)','Match into a family medicine residency through the NRMP','Complete a three-year (or longer) residency','Obtain a state medical license and board certification','Maintain certification with continuing education'])]],
['Medical school and licensing',[
'Medical school lasts **four years**. The first two years emphasize foundational sciences—anatomy, physiology, biochemistry, pharmacology, pathology—and clinical skills; the last two years are **clinical rotations (clerkships)** in family medicine, internal medicine, pediatrics, surgery, obstetrics and gynecology, psychiatry and electives. **MD** graduates take the **United States Medical Licensing Examination (USMLE)**; **DO** graduates take the **COMLEX-USA** (and may also take the USMLE). DO schools add **osteopathic manipulative treatment** and emphasize a holistic approach; a high proportion of DO graduates enter primary care. In the final year, students apply to residencies and participate in **the Match**, run by the **National Resident Matching Program (NRMP)**, which pairs applicants’ and programs’ rank lists.',
sort('MD or DO?',['MD (allopathic)','DO (osteopathic)'],[['Licensing exam: USMLE',0],['Licensing exam: COMLEX-USA',1],['Training in osteopathic manipulative treatment',1],['Doctor of Medicine degree',0],['Doctor of Osteopathic Medicine degree',1]])]],
['Residency and board certification',[
'**Family medicine residency** lasts **at least three years** (some programs are four years). Residents provide care in a **continuity clinic** with their own panel of patients and rotate through inpatient medicine, pediatrics, obstetrics, emergency medicine, surgery, geriatrics, behavioral health, sports medicine and community medicine. Many programs offer **rural tracks**, global health and procedural training. After residency, physicians obtain a **state medical license** and **board certification** by the **American Board of Family Medicine (ABFM)** or the **American Osteopathic Board of Family Physicians**. Certification is maintained through **continuing certification**: ongoing learning, knowledge assessments and quality improvement.',
chk('How long is a family medicine residency at minimum?',['Three years','One year','Seven years','Six months'],'Some programs extend to four years.')]],
['Dual degrees, fellowships and added qualifications',[
'Family physicians can broaden their careers. **Dual degrees** include the **MD/MPH** (public health), **MD/MBA** (business and leadership), **MD/PhD** (research) and degrees in health policy or education. After residency, optional **fellowships** (typically one year) include **sports medicine**, **geriatric medicine**, **hospice and palliative medicine**, **adolescent medicine**, **sleep medicine**, **addiction medicine**, **obstetrics**, **rural medicine**, **faculty development**, **integrative medicine** and **global health**. Some lead to **Certificates of Added Qualifications (CAQs)** from the ABFM—such as in adolescent medicine, geriatric medicine, hospice and palliative medicine, pain medicine, sleep medicine and sports medicine—showing expertise while remaining a family physician.',
match('Match the option to its focus',[['MD/MPH','Public health and population health'],['MD/MBA','Health care leadership and management'],['Sports medicine fellowship','Athletes and musculoskeletal injuries'],['Geriatric medicine CAQ','Care of older adults'],['Hospice and palliative medicine','Comfort and serious illness care'],['Faculty development fellowship','Teaching future physicians']])]],
['Practice settings and emphases',[
'Family physicians practice in **private and group practices**, **hospital-owned clinics**, **federally qualified health centers (FQHCs)** that serve underserved communities, **rural health clinics**, **academic medical centers**, **urgent care**, **emergency departments** (especially rural), **hospital medicine** (as hospitalists), **long-term care**, the **military**, the **Indian Health Service**, **public health departments**, **telehealth** and **direct primary care** (a membership model). Practice **emphases** let physicians tailor their work: **maternity care**, **behavioral health integration**, **procedures**, **LGBTQ+ health**, **sports medicine**, **global health** or **academic teaching and research**. This flexibility allows physicians to change settings over a career without retraining in a new specialty.',
kase('Shaping a career',{a:{text:'A resident loves both obstetrics and caring for rural communities. Which path fits?',choices:[['Family medicine with a rural track and maternity care emphasis or obstetrics fellowship','b','Correct. Rural family physicians often provide maternity care.',true],['Leave family medicine to become a dermatologist','b','That does not match either interest.',false]]},b:{text:'Ten years later, the physician wants to influence community health policy. Which option helps?',choices:[['Earn an MPH and work with the public health department','c','Correct. Dual training in public health supports policy leadership.',true],['Stop practicing medicine entirely','c','Family physicians can add roles without leaving clinical care.',false]]},c:{text:'The physician continues delivering babies part-time while serving as county health officer.',end:true}})]],
["Preparing now in high school and college",["Students can start building toward family medicine long before medical school. In **high school**, take rigorous science and math, practice writing and public speaking, join HOSA, volunteer in health settings, shadow physicians when possible and learn about pipeline or summer programs offered by local medical schools. In **college**, any major is acceptable if prerequisites are completed; strong applicants combine solid grades and MCAT scores with sustained **clinical experience**, **service** to underserved communities, **research or scholarship**, **leadership** and evidence of **teamwork and empathy**. Admissions committees increasingly use **holistic review**, considering life experiences, resilience and commitment to service alongside academics.","In **medical school**, students explore family medicine through **family medicine interest groups (FMIGs)**, student membership in the AAFP, clinical rotations, rural or underserved electives and AAFP’s national conference for students and residents. Strong letters from family physicians and a clear sense of why the specialty fits help in the Match. Sharing this roadmap gives peers concrete next steps—exactly what “span of medical education” means to a student audience.",{"t":"check","q":"What do medical school admissions committees use to consider experiences and commitment beyond grades?","options":["Holistic review","A lottery","Only MCAT scores","Only class rank"],"why":"Holistic review weighs academics with experiences and attributes."}]]
],takeaways:['Path: bachelor’s degree and MCAT, four years of MD or DO school, USMLE or COMLEX, the NRMP Match, a three-year residency and ABFM certification.','Options include MD/MPH, MD/MBA, MD/PhD and fellowships such as sports, geriatrics, palliative and addiction medicine; CAQs recognize added expertise.','Settings range from rural clinics and FQHCs to hospitals, academic centers, telehealth and direct primary care.'],terms:[['Residency','Graduate medical training after medical school.'],['The Match','NRMP process pairing applicants with residency programs.'],['Certificate of Added Qualifications','ABFM credential for expertise in an area like sports medicine.']],mcqs:[q('How long is a family medicine residency?','At least three years','One year','Seven years','No residency is required','Some programs last four years.'),q('Which exam do DO students take?','COMLEX-USA','MCAT only','NCLEX-RN','GRE','They may also take the USMLE.'),q('Which organization runs the residency Match?','National Resident Matching Program','AAFP','ABFM','HOSA','Applicants and programs submit rank lists.'),q('Which credential shows added expertise while remaining a family physician?','Certificate of Added Qualifications','A nursing license','A pharmacy degree','An associate degree','The ABFM awards CAQs.'),q('An MD/MPH dual degree adds training in:','Public health','Business','Law','Engineering','MPH stands for Master of Public Health.'),q('Which is a family medicine fellowship?','Sports medicine','Neurosurgery','Cardiothoracic surgery','Interventional radiology','Sports medicine is a common CAQ.'),q('Which setting serves underserved communities with federal support?','Federally qualified health center','Luxury spa','Cosmetic surgery center','Private research lab','FQHCs provide care regardless of ability to pay.'),q('Which test is required for most medical school applicants?','MCAT','SAT subject test','LSAT','NCLEX','The Medical College Admission Test.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a timeline for a high school student who wants to become a family physician, and describe at least four career options available after residency.',rubric:[['Undergraduate and admission',['bachelor','prerequisite','mcat','shadow','volunteer']],['Medical school and licensing',['four years','md','do','usmle','comlex','rotation']],['Residency and certification',['match','nrmp','three-year','residency','abfm','board']],['Career options',['fellowship','cap','caq','mph','sports','rural','fqhc','hospitalist']]],model:'In high school, the student should take strong science courses, join HOSA and shadow physicians. Next comes a four-year bachelor’s degree with biology, chemistry, physics, biochemistry, psychology and statistics, plus clinical volunteering and research, followed by the MCAT and applications to MD or DO schools. Medical school lasts four years: two years of foundational science and two years of clinical rotations, with the USMLE or COMLEX-USA licensing exams. In the final year, the student applies to family medicine residencies and enters the NRMP Match, then completes at least three years of residency with a continuity clinic and rotations across specialties, earns a state license and is board certified by the American Board of Family Medicine, maintaining certification through continuing learning. Afterward, options include practicing in a rural clinic or federally qualified health center, working as a hospitalist, completing a sports medicine or geriatrics fellowship that leads to a Certificate of Added Qualifications, earning an MPH to lead public health programs or teaching residents in academic medicine.'},sources:[RULES,ABFM,NRMP,AAFP]};

export const F5={title:'Work, lifestyle and financial implications (Objective c)',hook:'Family physicians describe deep relationships with patients and wide flexibility—and also heavy documentation, long hours early in their careers and the cost of medical school. Objective (c) asks for an honest, balanced picture of daily work, the team, lifestyle and money.',objectives:['Describe the benefits and challenges of a career in family medicine.','Explain the family physician’s role on the health care team.','Discuss work-life balance and burnout prevention.','Explain the cost of medical education, salaries, signing bonuses, scholarships and loan forgiveness.'],sections:[
['Benefits and challenges',[
'Family physicians often cite **long-term relationships** with patients and families, **variety** (no two days are alike), the ability to **prevent disease** rather than only treat it, **flexibility** in settings and schedules and the satisfaction of serving a community. Challenges include the breadth of knowledge required, **administrative burden**—electronic health record documentation, prior authorizations and insurance requirements—time pressure in short visits, compensation that is lower than many procedural specialties and the emotional weight of caring for patients through difficult illnesses. Present both sides: judges reward detailed, accurate descriptions, and peers trust a balanced picture.',
sort('Benefit or challenge?',['Benefit','Challenge'],[['Caring for families across generations',0],['Variety of patients and problems',0],['Extensive EHR documentation',1],['Flexibility to change practice settings',0],['Prior authorization paperwork',1],['Lower pay than some procedural specialties',1],['Preventing disease before it starts',0],['Short appointment times',1]])]],
['The family physician on the health care team',[
'Family physicians lead and collaborate with **interprofessional teams**. **Medical assistants** room patients, take vital signs and update records; **nurses** triage, educate and coordinate care; **nurse practitioners** and **physician assistants** provide care alongside physicians; **clinical pharmacists** manage complex medication regimens; **behavioral health clinicians** provide counseling in the same clinic; **care coordinators and social workers** connect patients with community resources; and **specialists** consult on complex problems. The family physician serves as the patient’s **medical home**, integrating information from every source and making sure nothing falls through the cracks. Strong communication—handoffs, team huddles and shared plans—improves safety.',
match('Match the team member to their role',[['Medical assistant','Rooms patients and records vital signs'],['Clinical pharmacist','Reviews and optimizes medications'],['Behavioral health clinician','Provides counseling within primary care'],['Care coordinator','Connects patients with services and follow-up'],['Specialist','Consults on complex conditions'],['Family physician','Integrates care as the medical home']])]],
['Lifestyle and work-life balance',[
'Schedules vary widely. Outpatient family physicians often work weekdays with some evening or weekend clinic hours and share **call** for after-hours questions; hospitalists may work blocks of shifts (for example, seven days on and seven off); physicians who deliver babies or work in rural hospitals have more unpredictable hours. Many choose **part-time** or flexible schedules, telehealth or direct primary care to control their workload. **Burnout**—emotional exhaustion, depersonalization and reduced sense of accomplishment—is a real risk in medicine, driven largely by administrative burden. Protective factors include team-based care, scribes and better EHR tools, supportive leadership, peer support, boundaries and personal wellness practices. Ask your interviewee how they protect time for family, health and hobbies.',
chk('Which factor is most often linked to physician burnout in primary care?',['Administrative and documentation burden','Too few patients','Too much vacation','Short training'],'Reducing paperwork and using team-based care help.')]],
['The cost of medical education',[
'Medical school is expensive. Tuition, fees and living costs for four years often total several hundred thousand dollars, and the **AAMC** reports that graduates who borrow have a **median education debt of roughly $200,000**. Students reduce costs with **scholarships** (school, community and organizational), attending in-state public schools, living frugally and service-based programs. The **National Health Service Corps (NHSC) Scholarship** pays tuition and a stipend in exchange for service in shortage areas; the military’s **Health Professions Scholarship Program** pays tuition in exchange for service; some states offer **primary care scholarships** and tuition-free tracks. Understanding these options can make family medicine more accessible.']],
['Salary, bonuses and loan forgiveness',[
'Family physicians earn a comfortable living—the **BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics** page for family medicine physicians lists current mean wages, which are well into six figures—though compensation is lower than in many procedural specialties. Pay varies by region, setting and workload; rural and underserved areas often pay more and add incentives. Employers commonly offer **signing bonuses**, relocation assistance, **residency stipends** and student loan help. Loan relief programs include **NHSC Loan Repayment** (service in a Health Professional Shortage Area), **Public Service Loan Forgiveness (PSLF)**—forgiveness of remaining federal Direct Loan balances after **120 qualifying monthly payments** while working full-time for a government or qualifying nonprofit employer—**Indian Health Service** loan repayment and many **state programs**. When presenting, use current, sourced figures and explain how debt can be managed.',
match('Match the program to how it helps',[['NHSC Scholarship','Pays tuition in exchange for service in shortage areas'],['NHSC Loan Repayment','Repays loans for service in an HPSA'],['Public Service Loan Forgiveness','Forgives remaining federal loans after 120 qualifying payments'],['Military HPSP','Pays medical school tuition in exchange for military service'],['Signing bonus','Employer payment for accepting a position'],['State primary care programs','Scholarships or repayment for serving in-state']]),
kase('A financial worry',{a:{text:'A peer says, “I could never afford medical school, so family medicine isn’t for me.” How do you respond?',choices:[['Explain scholarships, NHSC and military programs and loan forgiveness like PSLF, with sources','b','Correct. Accurate information can open the path.',true],['Agree that it is impossible','b','Many programs exist to reduce cost.',false]]},b:{text:'They ask whether family physicians earn enough to repay loans. What is accurate?',choices:[['Family physicians earn six-figure salaries, and repayment programs and bonuses help manage debt','c','Correct. Use current BLS data to show this.',true],['Family physicians earn minimum wage','c','This is inaccurate.',false]]},c:{text:'Your peer looks up the NHSC Scholarship and adds it to their college planning list.',end:true}})]],
["Practice models and how physicians are paid",["Family physicians work in several **practice models**. In **hospital- or health system–employed** practices, physicians typically receive a **salary** plus incentives, with the system handling business tasks. In **independent private practices**, physicians own the practice, have more control and take on business risk. **Federally qualified health centers** serve underserved patients regardless of ability to pay and often qualify physicians for NHSC loan repayment. **Direct primary care** practices charge patients a **monthly membership fee** instead of billing insurance for each visit, often allowing smaller panels and longer visits. **Academic** physicians combine patient care with teaching and research.","Payment methods shape daily work. **Fee-for-service** pays for each visit or procedure; productivity is often measured in **relative value units (RVUs)**. **Capitation** pays a set amount per patient per month regardless of visits. **Value-based** contracts add bonuses or shared savings for meeting quality goals such as blood pressure control, cancer screening and fewer avoidable hospital visits. Ask your interviewee which model they work in, how they are paid and how it affects their time with patients—real examples make objective (c) concrete for peers.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Fee-for-service or value-based/capitated?","buckets":["Fee-for-service","Value-based or capitated"],"items":[["Payment for each office visit billed",0],["Productivity measured in RVUs",0],["A set monthly amount per patient",1],["Bonus for meeting blood pressure control goals",1],["Shared savings for reducing avoidable admissions",1],["Payment for each procedure performed",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Benefits: relationships, variety, prevention and flexibility; challenges: administrative burden, time pressure and lower pay than procedural fields.','Family physicians lead interprofessional teams as the patient’s medical home; burnout is reduced by team-based care and boundaries.','Median debt for borrowers is roughly $200,000 (AAMC); NHSC, military HPSP, PSLF (120 payments), state programs and signing bonuses help.'],terms:[['Burnout','Exhaustion and detachment from chronic work stress.'],['Public Service Loan Forgiveness','Federal loan forgiveness after 120 qualifying payments in public service.'],['Signing bonus','A payment offered for accepting a job.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a commonly cited benefit of family medicine?','Long-term relationships with patients and families','No documentation','The highest pay in medicine','Working only one day a week','Continuity is rewarding.'),q('Which challenge is most associated with burnout?','Administrative and documentation burden','Too much patient variety','Too few patients','Short training','Paperwork drives burnout.'),q('PSLF forgives remaining federal loans after:','120 qualifying monthly payments in public service','One payment','Five years in any job','Graduation','It requires qualifying employment.'),q('The NHSC Loan Repayment Program requires:','Service in a Health Professional Shortage Area','Working in a private luxury clinic','No service','Military enlistment','It supports underserved areas.'),q('Who reviews and optimizes complex medication regimens on a primary care team?','Clinical pharmacist','Receptionist','Medical student only','Security officer','Pharmacists are key team members.'),q('Where can you find current wage data for family medicine physicians?','BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics','A social media post','A video game','A fashion magazine','Cite source and year.'),q('Which program pays tuition in exchange for military service?','Health Professions Scholarship Program','PSLF','Medicare','HOSA','The military HPSP covers tuition.'),q('Which statement about family physician lifestyle is accurate?','Schedules vary, and many choose flexible or part-time options','All work 100-hour weeks forever','None take call','None can work part-time','Settings shape schedules.')],frq:{prompt:'Present a balanced view of the work, lifestyle and financial realities of family medicine to students who worry about cost and burnout.',rubric:[['Benefits and challenges',['relationship','variety','prevention','documentation','administrative','challenge']],['Team and role',['team','nurse','pharmacist','medical home','coordinat']],['Lifestyle and burnout',['schedule','call','part-time','burnout','balance']],['Finances',['debt','scholarship','nhsc','pslf','signing','salary','bls']]],model:'Family physicians enjoy long-term relationships with patients across generations, variety in every day and the ability to prevent disease, and they can choose settings from rural clinics to hospitals and telehealth. The challenges are real: they must know a wide range of medicine, face heavy EHR documentation and prior authorizations, have short visits and earn less than many procedural specialties. They lead teams of medical assistants, nurses, NPs and PAs, pharmacists, behavioral health clinicians and care coordinators, serving as the patient’s medical home. Schedules vary—many work weekdays with shared call, hospitalists work blocks, and many choose part-time or flexible schedules—and burnout, driven mostly by paperwork, is reduced with team-based care, scribes, supportive leadership and personal boundaries. Medical school is costly, with a median debt of roughly $200,000 for borrowers according to the AAMC, but scholarships, the NHSC and military scholarship programs, signing bonuses, NHSC loan repayment and Public Service Loan Forgiveness after 120 qualifying payments make the path manageable, and BLS data show family physicians earn six-figure salaries.'},sources:[RULES,BLS_FM,NHSC,PSLF]};
